<%- include('partials/head') %>

<div class="page-head">
  <h1>Terms of Service</h1>
  <div class="meta">The rules for using BotApprove</div>
</div>

<div class="prose">
  <p class="updated">Last updated <%= updated %>. Operated by <%= operator %>.</p>

  <p>
    BotApprove is a Discord bot and web dashboard that screens bots joining your server. By adding
    it to a server, signing in to the dashboard, or paying for a subscription, you agree to these
    terms. If you do not agree, remove the bot and stop using the dashboard.
  </p>

  <h2>Who can use it</h2>
  <p>
    You must meet Discord's own minimum age requirement for your country and follow the
    <a href="https://discord.com/terms" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Discord Terms of
    Service</a>. To add BotApprove to a server you must have the Manage Server permission there,
    which means you are authorised to make that decision for that community.
  </p>
  <p>BotApprove is an independent project. It is not affiliated with, endorsed by, or operated by Discord.</p>

  <h2>What the service does, and what it does not promise</h2>
  <p>
    BotApprove removes bots that join your server and holds them for human approval. It is a
    safety tool, not a guarantee. It is provided <strong>as is</strong>, without warranty of any
    kind. In particular:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>It cannot stop a bot it lacks the permission or role position to remove.</li>
    <li>It depends on Discord's API and gateway, which can be slow, rate limited, or unavailable.</li>
    <li>Detection heuristics, including keyword matching and image analysis, produce false
      positives and false negatives.</li>
    <li>It does not protect against attacks carried out by human accounts, only by bots.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    You remain responsible for your server's security. Do not treat BotApprove as your only
    safeguard.
  </p>

  <h2>Acceptable use</h2>
  <p>Do not:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Submit a bot to the shared threat list knowing the report is false, or to harm a competitor
      or someone you have a dispute with.</li>
    <li>Use the auto-ban feature to target individuals for reasons unrelated to genuine nuke bot
      activity.</li>
    <li>Attempt to break, overload, reverse engineer, or gain unauthorised access to the service or
      its infrastructure.</li>
    <li>Resell or rebrand the service without written permission.</li>
    <li>Use the service to break Discord's terms, or any law that applies to you.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Actions taken against people</h3>
  <p>
    BotApprove can kick or ban the account that invited a bot on the shared threat list. That
    setting is <strong>off by default</strong> and you turn it on yourself. When you do, you are
    the one deciding to remove those people from your server, and you are responsible for that
    decision and for handling any appeals from your members. The operator is not a party to
    moderation disputes inside your community.
  </p>

  <h2>The shared threat list</h2>
  <p>
    Anyone running the bot can nominate a bot for the instance wide threat list. Submissions are
    reviewed manually and are <strong>never listed automatically</strong>. The operator decides
    what goes on the list, may remove entries at any time, and is under no obligation to accept,
    explain, or keep any submission. A listing is an operational judgement about software
    behaviour, not an accusation against any person.
  </p>

  <h2>Paid features</h2>
  <p>
    Core screening is free. Some features require a paid subscription or licence key. Which
    features sit behind payment can change, and prices can change, with reasonable notice for
    existing subscribers.
  </p>

  <h3>Refunds</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Payments are non-refundable.</strong> That includes partial periods after a
    cancellation, downtime, and cases where you stop using the service or lose access because of a
    breach of these terms. Cancelling stops future charges; it does not refund past ones.
  </p>
  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      If you are a consumer in the UK, EU, or another place with a statutory right to cancel a
      digital purchase, that right overrides this section and you keep it. By starting a
      subscription and using premium features immediately, you ask for the service to begin at
      once and accept that once it has been fully supplied within the cancellation window that
      right may no longer apply.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h3>If a payment lapses</h3>
  <p>
    Premium features switch off and the server owner is notified. Core screening keeps running,
    free, and BotApprove does not leave your server over money. Your settings, whitelist, and
    keyword list are kept, so paying again restores everything.
  </p>

  <h2>Suspension and termination</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Access can be suspended or terminated at any time, with or without notice</strong>,
    including for breaking these terms, abusing the threat list, non-payment, or putting the
    service or other users at risk. The operator may also discontinue the service entirely, or
    any part of it, at any time.
  </p>
  <p>
    You can stop at any point by removing the bot from your server. Doing so ends these terms for
    that server, other than the sections that by their nature survive, such as liability and
    refunds.
  </p>

  <h2>Liability</h2>
  <p>
    To the maximum extent the law allows, the operator is not liable for indirect or consequential
    loss, lost profit, lost data, or damage caused by a bot that BotApprove failed to stop, by a
    legitimate bot it removed, or by a person it kicked or banned on your instruction. Where
    liability cannot be excluded, it is limited to the greater of the amount you paid in the
    twelve months before the claim, or ten US dollars.
  </p>
  <p>Nothing here excludes liability that cannot legally be excluded, such as for fraud.</p>

  <h2>Changes</h2>
  <p>
    These terms may change. Material changes will be noted by the date at the top of this page.
    Continuing to use the service after a change means you accept it.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>
    Reach the operator through <a href="<%= contactUrl %>" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">GitHub</a>.
  </p>
</div>

